# Template for `deploy/app.env`, the only secret-bearing file in the deployment.
# Copy it on the instance and fill in the value; the copy is gitignored and excluded from
# the Docker build context, so it is neither pushed nor baked into an image layer:
#
#   cp deploy/app.env.example deploy/app.env
#   docker compose run --rm --no-deps --entrypoint '' app php artisan key:generate --show
#   # paste the printed value below, then: docker compose up -d --build
#
# `--show` prints the key instead of writing it into a `.env` that does not exist in the
# image. Any other source of 32 random bytes base64-encoded would do; this one is to hand.
#
# Everything else the container is configured with lives in `compose.yaml`, in the open.
# This file exists for the one value that cannot.

# Laravel's single symmetric encryption key. Not an API key and not a credential for any
# third party — this project talks to nothing. It encrypts the session cookie and the
# XSRF-TOKEN cookie, which is the whole of what it does here.
#
# GENERATE IT ONCE AND NEVER REGENERATE IT. A Player_Token exists only inside the
# server-side session, and the only thing linking a browser to its session is that
# encrypted cookie. Rotate the key and every session in the wild becomes undecryptable at
# once: with no accounts to recover through, every player is locked out of every game in
# progress, permanently (ADR-005, Req 12.10). If a rotation is ever genuinely needed,
# `config/app.php` reads `APP_PREVIOUS_KEYS` as a comma-separated list so the old cookies
# still decrypt through the changeover — which helps only if the rotation was planned.
APP_KEY=
